course: dictionary
lesson: 14472
title: Partitive words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A partitive word shows a part of something, or an amount of something. Words like «some of», «a piece of», «a slice of» and «a glass of» are partitives. They tell you how much, not just what.
explain: Look at «She ate a slice of cake.» The word slice tells you she did not eat the whole cake. Partitives are very useful for things we cannot count one by one, such as bread, milk and advice.
explain: In French, the partitive means "some". «Je voudrais du fromage» means "I would like some cheese". Du comes before food. Ask yourself: is this a part of something bigger?
fact: A partitive shows a part of a whole or an amount, such as «a piece of», «a loaf of» or «some of».
fact: «A loaf of bread» means one loaf, so we count the bread by the loaf, not by the crumb.
fact: Partitive phrases are used for things we cannot count one by one, like tea, advice and sand.
fact: In French, «du», «de la» and «des» are partitive articles, and they mean «some».
fact: «Du pain» means «some bread», and «de la confiture» means «some jam».
exercise: «I'd like a glass of milk, please.» Which phrase is the partitive? | milk, please | I'd like | *a glass of | a
exercise: «Je voudrais du fromage.» What does du mean here? | two | not | *some | the whole
exercise: Which phrase shows a part of a whole? | pizzas | the pizza | *a slice of pizza | three pizzas
test: «He gave me a piece of advice.» What does «a piece of» show? | a new kind of book | a broken plate | *one part or bit of something | a whole dozen
test: Which word is a French partitive article meaning «some»? | bon | le | *du | très
exam: Which sentence uses a partitive phrase? | The sun is very hot. | *She drank a cup of tea. | The dog barked loudly. | Tom ran fast.
